PRINT STUDIO FOR SCHOOLS · SAMPLE ASSIGNMENTS
Interpretation Briefs
Find-and-interpret projects that can't be outsourced to a chatbot: documented sources, an original print,
and a written artist statement — with the making process visible to the teacher.
Every brief follows the same arc: Find (research the tradition in the CultureSchool Atlas — cited, faculty-reviewed
entries that double as the works-cited list) → Interpret (create an original print in Print Studio in dialogue with
what you learned) → Own it (an artist statement naming what you borrowed and what you changed). Exports
carry the provenance credit line automatically.
BRIEF 01
A Symbol That Speaks: Adinkra
GRADES 6–8 · VISUAL ARTS or INTEGRATED SOCIAL STUDIES · 2–3 WEEKS
The find
Adinkra symbols from Ghana are a visual language: each mark carries a proverb or value, traditionally stamped
onto cloth with carved calabash stamps. Choose ONE Adinkra symbol from the Atlas. Document: what it looks
like, the proverb or meaning it carries, who historically made and wore Adinkra cloth, and one thing that surprised
you.
The interpretation
Design a symbol of your own for a value YOU hold — then build a stamped-cloth-style repeat print around it in
Print Studio, using what you learned about how Adinkra organizes symbols in a grid. Your symbol, Adinkra's
visual grammar.
Turn in
Your documented source notes • your finished print • an artist statement (150+ words): what your symbol means,
what you borrowed from the tradition, what you changed and why.
Standards: VA.68.H.1.3 (significance of artwork to its culture) · VA.68.S.1.3 (cultural references in personal artwork). Supports
F.S. 1003.42(2)(h).
BRIEF 02
Pattern From Process: Shibori
GRADES 6–12 · VISUAL ARTS · 2 WEEKS
The find
In Japanese shibori, the pattern is a record of what was done to the cloth before the indigo dye touched it —
bound, stitched, clamped, or folded, each technique leaves its own signature. Choose one shibori technique from
the Atlas (kanoko, itajime, arashi...). Document how the physical process creates the pattern, and where and
when the tradition developed.
The interpretation
Design a print where the pattern records a process — translate the idea of “resist” into your own rule (what stays
untouched, and why?). Use an indigo-family palette from the palette library and explain your rule in the statement.
Turn in

Source notes • finished print • statement (150+ words): your rule, the technique that inspired it, and how your
digital “resist” differs from cloth and dye.
Standards: VA.68.O.1.1 / VA.912 organizational-structure equivalents (structural elements & design principles) · VA.68.H.2.2
(utilitarian objects and human experience). Supports F.S. 1003.42(2)(i).
BRIEF 03
My Way: The Quilters of Gee’s Bend
GRADES 9–12 · VISUAL ARTS or AMERICAN HISTORY · 3 WEEKS
The find
In Gee’s Bend, Alabama, generations of African American women — including named artists like Loretta Pettway
and Annie Mae Young — built one of America’s great art traditions: improvisational quilts whose bold, off-grid
geometry now hangs in major museums. Choose ONE named quilter from the Atlas. Document: her life and
circumstances, how she described her own way of working, and what makes her compositions recognizable.
The interpretation
Compose an improvisational print answering her principles — work “your way”: break the grid deliberately, reuse
and recombine pattern blocks the way the quilters reused cloth, and let the composition show its decisions.
Turn in
Source notes • finished print • statement (250+ words): who she was, one principle of hers you adopted, one
place you departed from it, and why her work belongs in the story of American art.
Standards: VA.912 Historical & Global Connections strand · directly supports F.S. 1003.42(2)(h) African American history and
(2)(r) women’s contributions — both annually certified topics.
